  "content": {
    "id": "p51-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Let $Z$ have the standard normal distribution. Give numerical probabilities to 4 decimal places, using the table values below.\n\n| $z$ | $P(Z\\le z)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0.4$ | $0.6554$ |\n| $0.7$ | $0.7580$ |\n| $0.8$ | $0.7881$ |\n| $1.1$ | $0.8643$ |\n| $1.3$ | $0.9032$ |\n| $1.5$ | $0.9332$ |\n| $1.6$ | $0.9452$ |\n| $2.4$ | $0.9918$ |\n| $2.9$ | $0.9981$ |\n\nFor $z=5$, use $P(Z\\le5)\\approx1.0000$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p51-q12-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Use symmetry to explain why $P(Z\\le0)=0.5$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The normal curve is symmetric about $0$, so half of its total area lies to the left of $0$.",
          "worked": "The standard normal curve is symmetric about $Z=0$. Its total area is $1$, so the areas on either side of $0$ are equal. Therefore $P(Z\\le0)=\\frac12=0.5$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p51-q12-b",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence use $P(0<Z\\le a)=P(Z\\le a)-0.5$ to find:",
        "layout": "grid",
        "columns": 3,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p51-q12-b-i",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "i",
            "prompt": "$P(0<Z\\le1.3)$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$0.4032$",
              "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}P(0<Z\\le1.3)&=0.9032-0.5\\\\&=0.4032\\end{aligned}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 14
          },
          {
